$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{3-\sqrt{6x+21}}{x+2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3-\sqrt{6x+21}}{x+2}\cdot\dfrac{3+\sqrt{6x+21}}{3+\sqrt{6x+21}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the numerator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3^2-(6x+21)}{(x+2)(3+\sqrt{6x+21})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-6\cancel{(x+2)}}{\cancel{(x+2)}(3+\sqrt{6x+21})}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-6}{3+\sqrt{6x+21}} \text{, for }x\neq -2
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $-2$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
